act re-renders\n\nNothing to fix — the app is Server-Components-by-default per AGENTS.md, and the four `\"use client\"` components (`nav-rail.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `sign-out-control.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`) are small, have no unstable prop objects passed into memoized children, and `NAV`/`STAGES` are module-level consts, not re-created per render. Nothing to flag today.\n\n**Forward guidance for Phase 4** (conflict queue — the component that will actually have hundreds of rows and `j`/`k` keyboard nav): when you build `ConflictDiffRow` + the queue container, watch for:\n- Keeping the **queue container** as the client boundary and each `ConflictDiffRow` as a server-rendered child where possible — only the row that's focused/keyed needs client interactivity.\n- Wrapping the `j`/`k` key handler in the container, not per-row, so you're not attaching 400 keydown listeners.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- `web/lib/db/index.ts` already does the right thing (connection cached on `globalThis` across HMR reloads) — no change needed.\n- TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling per AGENTS.md — when you build that (Phase 4, `GET /api/runs/:runId/status`), set a `staleTime` matching your poll interval so React Query doesn't refetch on every focus/mount in addition to the interval, e.g.:\n```ts\nuseQuery({\n  queryKey: [\"run\", runId, \"status\"],\n  queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n  refetchInterval: 2000,\n  staleTime: 2000,\n});\n```\n- Engine-side, AGENTS.md already calls for prompt caching on the ruleset block in `agent/graph.py` — that's the single largest cost lever once the resolution agent is implemented (Phase 3). Nothing to add there beyond what's already specified.\n\n## 4. N+1 query patterns — none present, one to design against\n\n`engine/db.py` is currently all stubs. When you implement `write_entities`, `write_matches`, `write_conflicts`, `write_resolutions`, the natural trap is writing one `INSERT` per row in a loop (a run produces thousands of rows across these tables per AGENTS.md). Use `psycopg3`'s `executemany`/`COPY`, not a Python loop with one round-trip each:\n\n```python\ndef write_entities(run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    with get_conn() as conn, conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.executemany(\n            \"\"\"INSERT INTO entities (run_id, source_id, external_id, entity_type, fields,\n                                      parent_external_id, last_modified_at)\n               VALUES (%s, %s, %s, %s, %s, %s, %s)\"\"\",\n            [(run_id, e.source_id, e.external_id, e.entity_type, e.fields.model_dump_json(),\n              e.parent_external_id, e.last_modified_at) for e in entities],\n        )\n```\n\n`detect()`'s signature (`entities: dict[UUID, CanonicalEntity]`) is already the right shape to avoid N+1 lookups during the field-diff pass — keep that dict-based lookup rather than querying per-match when you implement it.\n\n## 5. Memory leaks\n\nNone found — no subscriptions, intervals, or event listeners without cleanup anywhere in the current client components. Nothing to flag.\n\n## 6. Redundant computations\n\nMinor, in the same `countSourcesBySide()`: it does three separate `.filter()` passes over `all`. Fine at current scale (a handful of configured sources), not worth optimizing into a single reduce until this list can realistically be large — which it won't be, since `sources` is admin-configured, not per-run data.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** the codebase doesn't have the bugs the prompt was fishing for yet, because the code that would contain them (pipeline stages, DB writer, conflict queue) hasn't been written. I applied the one real fix (`cache()` on `listSources`) — want me to make that edit, or leave the forward-guidance as notes for when you build those phases?\n",
